===Etymology 1===
Modification of upper case letter {{m|mul|C}}, from Etruscan {{m|ett|𐌂}}, from Ancient Greek {{m|grc|Γ||Gamma}}, from Phoenician {{m|phn|𐤂||gimel}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{audio|mul|Voiceless palatal plosive.ogg|Pronunciation of IPA {{IPAfont|[cɑː, ɑccɑː]}} with the sound {{IPAfont|[c]}}}}

====Letter====
{{letter|mul}}

# {{Latn-def|mul|letter|3}}

=====Usage notes=====
* Not to be confused with {{m|grc|ϲ}} (the lunate sigma).
* In many languages, the letter {{m|mul|c}} represents both a “hard” {{IPAchar|/k/}} sound and a “soft” sound ({{IPAchar|/s/}}, {{IPAchar|/ts/}}, {{IPAchar|/tʃ/}}, or {{IPAchar|/θ/}}), based on the following letter.
* In a number of languages, it is used only for the {{IPAchar|/tʃ/}} sound.<!-- Latin-based orthographies, not romanization of non-Latin scripts, such as with a number of West African languages for instance -->
* In many languages, it occurs frequently in the digraph with {{m|mul|ch}}.
* In some romanization systems of non-Latin scripts, it represents {{IPAchar|/tʃ/}}, {{IPAchar|/θ/}}, or {{IPAchar|/tsʰ/}}.

